# AIDE SESSION

Pipeline-position log for the in-flight feature. This is the canonical
cold-start protocol for the next agent reorienting on this project — it
captures the current architectural intent, the anti-regression invariants
every build phase must hold, and where the cycle paused. It is NOT
historical commentary; superseded design decisions are cut, not annotated.

Lifecycle: this file lives at `.aide/session.aide`, survives multiple
build/QA/fix cycles within a single feature lifecycle, and is deleted by
the maintainer agent only when the feature is fully completed and the
pipeline is no longer in use. See [.aide/docs/session-aide.md](./docs/session-aide.md)
for the full specification of this artifact.

---

## Current cycle: spec-bloat methodology refactor (set 2026-04-29, mid-rework)

Two intertwined workstreams in flight:

1. **Tighten the AIDE methodology itself** — add a Brevity Contract, introduce
   `brief.aide` as a per-module ephemeral pre-read, repurpose `session.aide`
   as the root pipeline-position log, flip `plan.aide` and `todo.aide`
   lifecycles to ephemeral (deleted post-QA by a maintainer agent), narrow
   the forbidden-content list to true implementation contracts and admit
   domain vocabulary explicitly, set whole-file size guidance (~150 target,
   ~175 refactor signal), and update `/aide:refactor` with a `--specs`
   batched cascade-aware sweep mode.

2. **Apply the tightened methodology to every existing spec in the repo** —
   wholesale top-down rewrite. Root and trunk are done; ~24 leaves remain.

The previous cycle (brain-config + update-playbook rework) landed before
this one. Its anti-regression invariants are listed below as the **current
architectural state of the brain layer**, which the slimmer leaf specs being
written this cycle must accurately reflect, not contradict.

---

## Architectural decisions landed this cycle

### 1. Brevity Contract (in `.aide/docs/aide-spec.md`)

- Hard caps: `description` ≤ 200 chars; `intent` ≤ 100 words;
  `outcomes.desired` and `outcomes.undesired` 3-6 items each, ≤ 2 sentences
  per item; `## Context` ≤ ~250 words; `## Strategy` ~7 decisions, ~80 words
  each; `## Good examples` and `## Bad examples` 2-3 each; whole-file target
  ~150 lines, refactor signal at ~175 lines.
- **Forbidden content (anywhere in `.aide`):** type signatures; file paths
  and folder layouts; argument-index or position-based contracts; schema
  cardinality assertions; migration history, deprecation notes, retired
  prior designs; long justifications/asides annotating the contract;
  function-name enumerations standing in for outcomes.
- **Domain vocabulary IS allowed:** entity names, status enum values, field
  names that name a domain concept, domain enum values, specific numbers
  tied to domain decisions. Test: if a non-engineer who knows the business
  would recognize the term, it belongs.

### 2. Level boundaries clarified

The user's load-bearing insight late in the session: the project root spec
should carry **pure business intent only** — what AIDE delivers as a product
to teams adopting it. Implementation choices belong one level down at the
trunk.

| Level | Carries | Avoids |
|---|---|---|
| `.aide/intent.aide` (root) | Pure business intent — what teams adopting AIDE actually GET, why this project exists from the user's perspective | "Two delivery channels," "progressive disclosure," "idempotency," "MCP tool + npx CLI," any commitment to a particular delivery mechanism |
| `src/.aide` (trunk) | Implementation/delivery shape — how the server is built to deliver business value: two channels, no-overlap tool surface, dual-entry install service, client parity, brain-agnostic runtime | Restating root business commitments |
| Leaves | Each leaf's own slice — only what's specific to that submodule | Restating parent invariants, sibling-redundant content |

The reference for "what good looks like" is
`D:\Code\Me\cold-outreach-analyzer\src\service\outreach\sequence\.aide`
(~150 lines, decision-form Strategy, tight outcomes). Inside this repo, the
calibration target post-refactor is `src/tools/discover/.aide` (already
swept clean).

